WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.439 --> 00:00:07.680
Experimental self-driving vehicles
are taking to our streets, skies and seas

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.980
powered by artificial intelligence.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.460
They might transport you around cities,

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.300
grow your food, or deliver packages to your doorstep.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.740
They could reveal hidden glimpses of the
world around you

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.320
and even save lives.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:26.420
They are designed to outperform humans

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:29.940
at specialised tasks, in very specific environments.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.720
But they are not quite ready
to take full control.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:39.920
Training their AI to interact safely with us in our
complex and unpredictable world

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:42.480
is still a significant challenge.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:47.400
Deciding how and whether they should be used is another.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:58.760
Self-driving vehicles use sensors and
artificial intelligence, or AI.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:04.400
AI is what enables self-driving
vehicles to react in unpredictable situations.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:09.460
This is an early example of British self-driving technology.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:12.920
The car was modified by the road research laboratory,

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:16.380
which added an experimental automatic steering system.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:20.820
The experiments were carried out on a specially adapted test track.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:25.800
When the automatic steering mode was engaged, the driver could take their hands off the wheel.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.460
This car was a prototype and was never commercially produced.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:37.060
At the time, researchers estimated such systems could allow 50% more cars to drive on the roads,

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:39.520
while reducing accident rates.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:46.820
Today, PODs are being used to help carry
passengers through airports and car parks.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:50.360
This is an example that researchers are testing in Greenwich.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.480
Where it could soon take visitors on a scenic journey around the peninsula.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:59.100
Quiet and fully electric,  it's meant to operate in public spaces

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:02.620
alongside pedestrians, cyclists and animals.

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:08.320
Self-driving technology is being developed for many types of land vehicles.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:14.080
From cars and trucks to military supply vehicles and robots that make deliveries

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:15.940
and farm crops.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:20.720
'Tom' is the first prototype developed by the small robot company.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:27.020
It can decide what route to take around
a field, photograph crops and tag their position on a map

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.599
It works alongside 'Dick' and 'Harry' which pull weeds and water the crops.

00:02:31.599 --> 00:02:35.840
This data is sent to an artificial
intelligence-driven control system

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:36.840
called 'Wilma.'

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:43.120
Technology developers argue self-driving
vehicles could make journeys

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:45.900
faster, safer and more sustainable.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.800
But some environments such as busy cities

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:54.960
are highly unpredictable and would need significant modifications to enable self-driving technology.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:58.060
Your behaviour might have to change too.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:05.700
Remote-controlled drones, piloted by
human operators, have been around for decades.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:10.820
Now, experimental drones that fly themselves are taking to the skies.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:17.860
Programming drones to fly on their own allows human operators to many at a time

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:20.180
and send them where humans cannot go.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:26.820
As young boys in Afghanistan, brothers 
Massoud and Mahmud Hassani

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.780
would make and race wind-powered toys.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:34.300
Today,  their goal is to create an
unmanned de-mining system

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:38.100
that could clear the world's landmines within in 10 years.

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:43.320
Massoud first created
the Mine Kafon  ball 2011.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:46.040
Roughly the height and weight of
a person,

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:50.840
the full-sized art piece catches enough
wind to roll it over minefields.

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:54.520
Detonating hidden landmines as it goes.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:59.519
Many types of self-driving water
vehicles are in development.

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.900
From small ships that form footbridges
on demand

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:08.060
to massive cargo ships and military
vessels that hunt for hidden submarines.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:14.700
Some are being developed to explore
challenging deep ocean environments

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:17.580
and beneath sea ice in polar regions.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:23.680
In these extreme places, far beyond the
limits of human endurance and communication,

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:29.340
researchers must rely on these vehicles to take full control over their missions.

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:35.520
We know more about the surface of Mars
than our ocean floor.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:39.260
These technologies could help make our world a smaller place.

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:41.800
They might also help us protect it.

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:48.780
This is a two-thirds scale model of the auto sub long-range vehicles.

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:55.000
engineers at The National Oceanography Centre have developed the ALR fleet to explore the deep ocean

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:56.760
and beneath polar ice,

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.240
collecting data on the causes and impacts of climate change.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.100
Each ALR  is equipped with intelligent
navigation systems

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:10.020
to ensure they can operate independently
for months at a time,

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:14.240
up to six thousand metres under water
and two thousand kilometers away

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:16.980
before returning safely with valuable data.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:29.800
Driverless cars have been a dream for decades but until now have mostly been science fiction.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:32.360
This technology is developing quickly.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.680
And in the coming years, we will increasingly see driverless cars and drones in our public spaces.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:42.100
When that happens, we will all have decisions to make

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:45.280
about how we think these vehicles should be used.

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:47.960
And all of this will raise the question;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:50.700
Who is in Control?

